\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A central sign link on the Hurwitz circle}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{On the one-vertex cellular circle, take the integral
Hurwitz fiber and the link #{u=-1\in 2T}. Its inverse
transport acts by #{-\bbI} on the coefficient fiber.
The [cochain operators](fdip-00SR), in degree order #{0,1},
have matrices
##{\extD=\begin{pmatrix}0&-2\bbI_4\\-2\bbI_4&0\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad
 K=\begin{pmatrix}m\bbI_4&-2\bbI_4\\-2\bbI_4&m\bbI_4\end{pmatrix}.}
The cochain form is the block sum of two copies of the
[integral Hurwitz form](fdip-00V2). By \ref{fdip-00V6},
#{\pfaffian(B)=1} and #{BK} is alternating.}

\p{Block elimination over #{\mathbb Q(m)} gives
#{\det K=(m^2-4)^4}. Both sides belong to #{\mathbb Z[m]},
so this is an integral polynomial identity. The quotient
#{p_B(K)=\pfaffian(BK)} is also an integer polynomial.
By \ref{fdip-00UX}, its square is #{(m^2-4)^4}.
Since #{\mathbb Z[m]} is a domain, it equals one of
#{\pm(m^2-4)^2}. Its leading coefficient is one: choosing
the mass term in all #{4} matching pairs gives
#{m^4\pfaffian(B)=m^4}. Hence
##{p_B(K)=(m^2-4)^2,\qquad\det K=(m^2-4)^4.}
At mass #{3}, these values are #{25} and #{625}; at mass
#{2}, both vanish. Scalar extension preserves these identities,
including in characteristic #{2}.}
